  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/65b74168f1165014df494c238dc400ab772f2c2486935ba6b78fdcbc6658b3b6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/3b77cdfa4958cd1892c31baf858b5f8cbe29e00e8e2a45b698a43c5ddae6198e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =y \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=0$ divide the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=y.\n$$",
